LLM-based AI agents are increasingly integrated into online information environments such as social media and discussion threads, transforming how users assess information trustworthiness [2]. In these spaces, AI agents act as active participants that generate answers, summaries, and explanations, influencing trustworthiness judgements, as both informational authorities and social actors. While prior work has examined how users evaluate trustworthiness of online information in human-only or agent-only settings [3], there is limited understanding of how these judgements are formed in mixed human-AI groups, where human and AI-generated cues jointly shape how information is interpreted and acted upon. A key mechanism underlying how users assess information trustworthiness is social conformity, whereby individuals tend to adjust their own judgements to align with the majority view, which in turn shapes their subsequent engagement behaviours [3]. While prior work in human-only or agent-only groups has identified contextual (e.g., majority–minority group difference, majority opinion, task objectivity) and personal (e.g., self-confidence, demographics, and trust-related dispositions) determinants of conformity [1], their role in mixed environments remains unclear. This is particularly crucial as AI-specific factors i.e., presence, position, and persona of agents can alter conformity dynamics. To address this research gap, this thesis examines how users judge trustworthiness of online information in mixed human-AI environments. The following research questions guide this study: •RQ1. How do contextual and personal determinants of social conformity influence perceived trustworthiness judgements in mixed environments? •RQ2. How do AI-related determinants influence conformity and perceived trustworthiness judgements in mixed environments? By addressing these research questions, this work aims to explain when and why AI agents amplify, weaken, or alter social influence. These insights can inform the design of agents that promote more critical and reliable trustworthiness judgements rather than triggering blind conformity. The research adopts a multi-study approach comprising four user studies across diverse group settings. In this thesis, conformity is defined as the change in users' self-reported trustworthiness judgements after exposure to group opinions, relative to their initial judgements. A change is considered conformity only when the shift moves towards majority opinion. The completed study examined perceived trustworthiness of news articles in a simulated social media environment, where participants assessed real and fake articles before and after exposure to human comments and AI opinions. Findings show that conformity increased when the AI agent supported the majority and decreased when it opposed it. Authoritative AI agents produced stronger effects than peer-style agents, particularly when aligned with the majority. Larger majority-minority group differences and critical majority opinions ( i.e. majority claims article is fake) further increased both likelihood and magnitude of conformity. Among personal factors, higher initial confidence reduced conformity, while greater trust in AI increased it. Conformity also shaped downstream behaviours, with critical majorities reducing react, comment, and share intentions while increasing reporting, with the opposite pattern under supportive majorities ( i.e. majority claims article is real). Future studies will examine effects of age and age-related stereotypes, AI communication styles, and AI errors. Together, these studies investigate how contextual, personal, and AI-related factors shape conformity and perceived trustworthiness judgements in mixed human-AI environments. Insights from this thesis can inform the design of online platforms that support more critical and reliable trustworthiness judgements.